\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Three-level synchronization hierarchy. \textbf  {Level~1 (Passive):} Coupling strength and mode selection are fixed by the physical interface (membrane, receptors); $\tau _{\mathrm  {sync}}$ is determined entirely by coupling physics. Example: \textit  {E.~coli} chemotaxis. \textbf  {Level~2 (Active):} Circuit-level gain modulation adjusts coupling strength in real time, making $\tau _{\mathrm  {sync}}$ a variable under partial control. Example: \textit  {C.~elegans} thermotaxis. \textbf  {Level~3 (Selective):} A selector mechanism dynamically allocates finite synchronization resources across multiple environmental channels, amplifying some and suppressing others. Example: cortical attention. Progression from molecular-level anticipation to reflective modeling follows Igamberdiev's framework \cite  {igamberdiev2015,igamberdiev2017}.}}{14}{figure.21}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:hierarchy}{{2}{14}{Three-level synchronization hierarchy. \textbf {Level~1 (Passive):} Coupling strength and mode selection are fixed by the physical interface (membrane, receptors); $\tsync $ is determined entirely by coupling physics. Example: \textit {E.~coli} chemotaxis. \textbf {Level~2 (Active):} Circuit-level gain modulation adjusts coupling strength in real time, making $\tsync $ a variable under partial control. Example: \textit {C.~elegans} thermotaxis. \textbf {Level~3 (Selective):} A selector mechanism dynamically allocates finite synchronization resources across multiple environmental channels, amplifying some and suppressing others. Example: cortical attention. Progression from molecular-level anticipation to reflective modeling follows Igamberdiev's framework \cite {igamberdiev2015,igamberdiev2017}}{figure.21}{}}
